Z||000005||Formula88||12-24-2001||10:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fierowrecker:<BR><B>If they come back with "FREEDOM OF SPEACH", say it doesn't mean "FREEDOM TO HATE"...<BR>crash...</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Actually, yes it does.  I can hate anyone I want.  I can make my hatred known.  What I can't do is express my hatred in a way that harms someone else.  But if I want to say all whites are superior to blacks (or blacks superior to whites), then that is my right. <P>That's how the KKK can get parade permits to promote their views.  It is protected free speech.  What makes me wonder sometimes is the double-standard with race.  Why is it if  black man says, "Black Power!" he's showing ethinic pride, but if a white man says, "White Power!" he's a racist?<P>Now before someone tries to peg me as supporting the KKK, I'm not.  But I am proud to be white, just as anyone should be proud to be who they are (not just based on color).<BR>The argument about black repression and slavery doesn't hold a lot of water with me.  I've never been a slave owner, and no black man I've ever heard of has been a slave.  Is there racism and prejudice?  Yes.  Does it need to be addressed?  Yes.  But the people who say that the white man owes the black man becasue of past sins is full of it, IMO.<P>I don't believe we'll get rid of racism as long as people are so concerned with equal rights and protection for races.  Only when we stop thinking about it altogether and race becomes a non-issue will racism die.  When we cease to be Anglo-Americans, Japanese-American, African-Americans, and just become Americans.  Then we'll have made some progress.  (That same idea works around the globe, 'cept for the "American" part.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Return of Fiero||12-24-2001||10:55 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> Actually, yes it does.  I can hate anyone I want.  I can make my hatred known.  What I can't do is express my hatred in a way that harms someone else.  But if I want to say all whites are superior to blacks (or blacks superior to whites), then that is my right....<P>&gt;Very true, and good point. and like you said, it is within freedom of speach. <P>What makes me wonder sometimes is the double-standard with race.  Why is it if  black man says, "Black Power!" he's showing ethinic pride, but if a white man says, "White Power!" he's a racist?...<P>&gt;Honestly, to belive anyone is superior to another due to heritage of any sort is still a supremis belief. The Black Panthers showed that, as well as the KKK.<P>But I am proud to be white, just as anyone should be proud to be who they are (not just based on color)....<P>&gt; Another true point. Everyone should be proud of who they are. It's how we become diverse. Even the jerk you can't get along with at work is someone who makes the world a little different, and thus more interesting. You might want to avoid this jerk, but diversity is nessisary, and being yourself makes us as a whole diverse. So you should be proud to be who you are.<P>I've never been a slave owner, and no black man I've ever heard of has been a slave.  Is there racism and prejudice?  Yes.  Does it need to be addressed?  Yes.  But the people who say that the white man owes the black man becasue of past sins is full of it, IMO....<P>&gt; Antother place I will side with you. If someone owed my grandfathers grandfather $1K, I dont belive that man who owes the money should have his greatchilds grandchild pay me that debt, that I personaly wasnt owed.<P>I don't believe we'll get rid of racism as long as people are so concerned with equal rights and protection for races.  Only when we stop thinking about it altogether and race becomes a non-issue will racism die.  When we cease to be Anglo-Americans, Japanese-American, African-Americans, and just become Americans.  Then we'll have made some progress.  (That same idea works around the globe, 'cept for the "American" part.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>&gt;If everyone left the issue alone, I could see this issue dropping. The only problem with that idea is most people are taught to hate. Back in WWII, Americans were taught to hate people from Japan. Slang terms were thrown from people who had never beed racial in there lives. They stoped buying at the normal corner store, because it was owned by a native of Japan. In some cases, people who were known to be American citizens by birthright were getting flack. (i know thats obvious)<P>Still, I have a hard time to take this all lightly. By your writing sytle, it almost seems thats your advice. Before this looks bad, let me say this. Of course, I am not you, there for it may just be my interpitation of your writing that has given me that opinion of your letter.<P>BTW, today, i noticed someone has scratched most of the writing on the bench out.<P>again, thanks for the space,<BR>Myke||||reg||1
